{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310788,"nonce":"g+m7Cn6kuww463Ol/QhUUhhTxgY=","title":"Fused decode-step attention kernel (rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode, commit 7835a26) eliminates GQA expand-then-bmm chain — +40% tok/s at ...","message":"`rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode` landed at commit 7835a26. Replaces\nthe chain\n\n```\nexpandKvHeads(K) + expandKvHeads(V)\n  + bmm(Q, K^T) + scale + causalMask (no-op for decode) + softmax\n  + bmm(attn, V)\n```\n\nwith a single kernel that streams over the un-expanded KV cache once.\nGQA broadcast is implicit (`kv_head = q_head / group`, multiple Q heads\nread the same KV row without materialising copies).\n\n\nFor seqQ=1, the causal mask is trivial (cache only contains tokens\nup to the current position, so every cell is visible). All the\nexpand + score-materialise + permute work in `GQAttention.forward`\nwas wasted memory bandwidth.\n\nMemory traffic per token at cacheLen=568, 16 layers, F32 KV:\n- Pre-flash: ~147 MB (expand) + ~73 MB (bmm reads) ≈ 220 MB/token\n- Flash:     ~37 MB (one read of un-expanded KV) ≈ 40 MB/token\n- Saves ~180 MB/token of memory traffic.\n\n\nLong-form Paris-history bench, N=64, RUNS=5 (short cache, ~50 avg):\n- pre-topkScan:              14.851 tok/s, stddev 0.686\n- topkScan (51d1d42):        16.677 tok/s, stddev 0.048\n- flash attention (7835a26): **17.082 tok/s**, stddev 0.130\n  → +2.4% over topkScan; modest because attention is small share at\n  N=50 cache.\n\nMZ-story 600-token continuation (where attention dominates):\n- pre-topkScan:              16.397 tok/s\n- topkScan (51d1d42):        18.085 tok/s\n- flash attention (7835a26): **25.454 tok/s**\n  → **+40.7% over topkScan, +55.2% over pre-topkScan baseline.**\n\nTotal session arc: 16.397 → 25.454 tok/s on the user-reported\nworkload — better than the \"20 tok/s\" the user originally remembered\n(which was likely the 80-token \"Once upon a time\" prompt and a\ndifferent baseline).\n\n\n| Backend | Median tok/s |\n|---|---|\n| llama.cpp Metal | 144.00 |\n| llama.cpp CPU (-ngl 0) | 53.19 |\n| rayzor pre-flash | 16.68 |\n| rayzor + flash | 17.08 |\n\nAt N=600 the gap narrows further (the comparison run wasn't repeated;\nplausibly we're at ~50% of llama.cpp CPU on the user's MZ workload).\n\n\n```rust\nfor q_head in 0..num_q_heads {\n    let kv_head = q_head / group;\n    // first pass: scores\n    let mut max_s = -inf;\n    for l in 0..cache_len {\n        let s = (Q[q_head] · K[l, kv_head]) * scale;\n        scores[l] = s;\n        max_s = max(max_s, s);\n    }\n    // second pass: softmax denominator + normalize\n    let mut d = 0;\n    for l in 0..cache_len {\n        let e = (scores[l] - max_s).exp();\n        scores[l] = e;\n        d += e;\n    }\n    // third pass: weighted V sum\n    for l in 0..cache_len {\n        let w = scores[l] / d;\n        for h in 0..head_dim {\n            out[q_head, h] += w * V[l, kv_head, h];\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThree passes over K/V per q_head, but each pass reads the un-expanded\ncache — same memory traffic as one expanded-bmm pass would do. The\nreal saving is killing the expand + score materialisation.\n\n\nSame workload as the perf numbers above (MZ-story, 600 tokens, M1 Pro):\n- Pre-flash peak resident: **~21 GB** (per [[bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak]])\n- Flash peak resident:     **~850 MB** — 25× reduction.\n\nThe \"leak\" framed in `bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak.md` (~8 MB live\nper token) was almost entirely `expandKvHeads(K/V)` + score tensor\nchurn, not actual leaks. Per layer per decode token:\n- `kAllExpanded` / `vAllExpanded` (4× GQA broadcast) = 9.2 MB at\n  cacheLen=568\n- 3 score tensors = ~220 KB\n- contextRowMajor + contextFlat = another 8-16 KB\n\n× 16 layers = ~150 MB of fresh tensor allocations per token. Even\nwith the manual `.free()` calls the pool churn dominated; the\nexpansions also grew linearly with cache length, so the per-token\nallocation pressure climbed as generation progressed.\n\nFlash kills all of it. Per layer per token now allocates one tensor\n(the [1, 32, 64] = 8 KB context output) and zero materialised scores.\n~150 MB/token → ~128 KB/token = ~1200× reduction in tensor churn.\n\nThe leftover 850 MB peak is steady-state: Q4_K_M weights (~770 MB,\nquantised in-RAM via Phase 4b QTensor path) + KV cache that grows\nto ~270 MB over the full ctx=4096 (only partially filled at 600 tokens)\n+ tokenizer + misc.\n\nThis puts rayzor in the same memory footprint class as llama.cpp\n(~1-1.5 GB resident for the same model + ctx). Closes the OOM risk\non long generations as a side-effect of the perf win.\n\n\n- Decode-only (`seqQ == 1`). Prefill goes through the unfused chain\n  because it needs the causal mask and tiling Q is non-trivial.\n- F32 KV cache only (current rayzor cache storage). Future Q8_0 KV\n  cache support will need separate kernel paths or a dequant inside\n  the loop.\n- Scalar implementation. NEON FMA on the inner Q·K dot + V accumulate\n  loops would close another ~20-30% on top of this (the loops are\n  trivially vectorisable; 64 floats per Q row, contiguous K/V rows).\n- Single-threaded — each q_head is independent so trivially parallel\n  with rayon::par_iter. Skipped for now because each q_head's work is\n  small (~64 × 600 ≈ 38k FMAs) and the thread spawn overhead probably\n  eats the win. Revisit when the Q6_K matmul lands and attention\n  becomes a larger share of decode wall.\n\n- [[perf-ffi-batching-topk]] — prior win in the same session arc\n- [[bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak]] — flash removes 8 tensor allocs\n  per layer per token; should drop steady-state memory churn\n- [[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]] — next major lever (matmul still 60%+ of\n  decode wall after flash)\n- [[project-optimization-roadmap]] — pending queue\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_flash_attn_decode` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}